1 ATTACH
    ATTACH gives the terminal to another process of the console's job and
    waits until it comes back, as DCL's ATTACH and a program's LIB$ATTACH
    do.  The process must be waiting for the terminal itself: a process
    that SPAWNed a subprocess and waits for it, or one that gave the
    terminal away by ATTACH.  A SPAWN/NOWAIT subprocess, the process
    typing ATTACH, and a process of another job are refused
    (%LIB-F-ATTREQREF); a process that isn't there is %SYSTEM-W-NONEXPR.

    Syntax:

        ATTACH process-name
        ATTACH/IDENTIFICATION=pid

    The terminal comes back when another process ATTACHes to the waiting
    one, or when the process that has it logs out (it then goes to that
    process's parent).  DCL then says so (%DCL-S-RETURNED).  A SPAWN that
    waits is over, its subprocess still there, when the subprocess
    ATTACHes back to its parent.

    Example:

        VAX> SPAWN
        $ ATTACH SYSTEM
        %DCL-S-RETURNED, control returned to process SYSTEM
        VAX> ATTACH SYSTEM_1
        %DCL-S-RETURNED, control returned to process SYSTEM_1
        $ LOGOUT
